From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:30:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi-scripts: keep the RSNO2E off on an FT BSS

A fast BSS transition keeps the AKM and the pairwise cipher of the
initial association (IEEE 802.11-2024 12.7.1.6.1). The target AP
rejects an FT authentication with another pairwise cipher with
status 42. f51374894635 ("wifi-scripts: SAE-EXT-KEY and GCMP-256 in
the EHT RSNO2E") puts both items into the RSNE Override 2 element of
an EHT BSS, and an AP without EHT sends no such element. A mobility
domain with EHT and non-EHT APs therefore splits in two. A client
that takes SAE-EXT-KEY and GCMP-256 on an EHT AP cannot roam with FT
to a non-EHT AP. Some clients then repeat the FT authentication
instead of a fresh SAE authentication and stay on the EHT AP.

A single-link BSS with ieee80211r offers the items of the element
only when sae_ext_key or gcmp256 asks for them. Every AP of the
mobility domain then offers the same set by default. An explicit 1
on every AP keeps the domain uniform too, with both items in the
RSNE. An AP MLD keeps the element, because all of its links share
the AKM that the element holds (IEEE 802.11be-2024 12.6.2, WPA3
Specification v3.5 2.5 item 4).

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 src/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc |   17 +++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/src/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc
+++ b/src/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc
@@ -117,8 +117,15 @@ export function parse_encryption(config,
 	 * the AKM and the pairwise cipher of the RSNO2E makes the RSNO2E a
 	 * duplicate, so the BSS sends none. An AP MLD sends none only where that
 	 * holds on every link (ap.uc mld_rsno2()).
+	 *
+	 * A fast BSS transition keeps the AKM and the pairwise cipher of the
+	 * initial association (IEEE 802.11-2024 12.7.1.6.1), and an AP without
+	 * EHT sends no RSNO2E. A single-link BSS with ieee80211r therefore offers
+	 * the RSNO2E items only when the option asks for them, so that every AP
+	 * of the mobility domain offers the same set.
 	 */
 	let eht = wildcard(dev_config?.htmode ?? '', 'EHT*');
+	let ft = !!config.ieee80211r && !config.mlo;
 	let compat = (config.auth_type == 'sae-compat');
 	let rsno2_mode = config.auth_type in [ 'sae', 'psk3', 'sae-mixed', 'psk3-mixed' ] ||
 		(!!config.mlo && config.auth_type == 'psk2');
@@ -128,10 +135,12 @@ export function parse_encryption(config,
 	 * password exists (ap.uc mld_rsno2()). sae-compat adds SAE-EXT-KEY to the
 	 * RSNO2E itself unless sae_ext_key is 0. */
 	let rsno2_sae = config.mlo ? (rsno2_mode || (compat && config.sae_ext_key === false)) :
-		(eht && rsno2_mode && config.sae_ext_key !== false);
-	let rsno2_pairwise = (config.gcmp256 !== false && phy_features?.cipher_gcmp256) ? 'GCMP-256' : 'CCMP';
-	config.gcmp256 ??= compat && eht;
-	config.sae_ext_key ??= compat && eht;
+		(eht && rsno2_mode &&
+			(ft ? config.sae_ext_key === true : config.sae_ext_key !== false));
+	let rsno2_gcmp256 = ft ? config.gcmp256 === true : config.gcmp256 !== false;
+	let rsno2_pairwise = (rsno2_gcmp256 && phy_features?.cipher_gcmp256) ? 'GCMP-256' : 'CCMP';
+	config.gcmp256 ??= compat && eht && !ft;
+	config.sae_ext_key ??= compat && eht && !ft;
 
 	switch(config.auth_type) {
 	case 'owe':
